      # ArchMap — Build Guide (Reframed)

**Design-System & Architecture Drift Detector for Frontend Repositories**

> ArchMap watches a React/Next.js TypeScript codebase and tells you where it's drifting from its own established patterns — styling that's fallen out of sync with the design system, and architectural shortcuts that break the intended data flow — with evidence tied to specific files and commits, not vague impressions.

This is the reframed version of the project. The original framing was "map the architecture of any repo" with styling as an extension bolted on at the end. That's the crowded lane — import-graph tools already exist (madge, dependency-cruiser, CodeSee, Sourcegraph). **The reframe puts deviation detection at the center**, and architecture mapping becomes the supporting evidence layer that makes deviation detection trustworthy, rather than the other way around.

---

## 1. The One-Line Idea

> **ArchMap establishes what "normal" looks like in a codebase — architecturally and stylistically — then flags where reality has drifted from it, with evidence.**

Not: "here's a graph of your repo" (a one-time novelty).
Instead: "here's what's inconsistent right now, why it matters, and when it was introduced" (an ongoing utility, the kind of thing that earns a permanent spot in a team's PR checks).

---

## 2. Why This Reframe Fixes the "Reinventing the Wheel" Problem

| Concern | Existing tools | ArchMap's actual gap to fill |
|---|---|---|
| Import/dependency graphing | madge, dependency-cruiser, Sourcegraph, CodeSee | Commodity. Don't lead with this. |
| Visual regression (pixel diffs) | Chromatic, Percy | Detects *pixels changed*, not *why* — no reasoning about design-system conformance |
| Token management | style-dictionary | Manages tokens you already declared; doesn't detect where code silently ignores them |
| "Explain my repo" LLM tools | various GPT-wrapper repo explainers | No persistent baseline, no evidence discipline, prone to hallucination |
| **Continuous drift detection tied to git evidence** | **Nobody doing this well for frontend styling + architecture together** | **This is ArchMap's wedge** |

The senior-dev objection ("feels like reinventing the wheel") applies squarely to plain architecture mapping. It does **not** apply to "continuously catch when a PR introduces styling/architecture inconsistency, and explain why it matters" — that's a CI-native product, not a one-off visualizer, and it's a much smaller, less-served niche.

---

## 3. Core Philosophy (Unchanged, Still the Foundation)

```
Code → Evidence → Relationships → Reasoning → Drift / Deviation
```

Two responsibilities, kept strictly separate:

| Layer | Responsibility | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| **Deterministic analysis** | Mechanically gathered facts | files, imports, style values in use, git history |
| **AI reasoning** | Interpreting evidence | "this drift matters because it's in a user-facing billing component," "this bypass of the service layer is risky because 4 other pages depend on that context" |

The agent never invents facts a tool can inspect directly — it only reasons over evidence tools hand it, and it never claims a deviation exists without a tool-verified baseline comparison behind it.

---

