## 7. Build Phases (Reordered Around the Reframe)

Deviation detection now ships early — by Phase 5 you have a usable MVP, not Phase 9.

### Phase 0 — Scaffolding
Mastra project, TS config, Zod schemas, folder structure. **Exit:** builds and runs.

### Phase 1 — `repo/`
`clone-repo.ts`, `cleanup-repo.ts`. Fix the current Zod `outputSchema` issue here (`z.object({...})`, not a plain object). **Exit:** clone a real repo reliably into `/tmp/archmap/<repo-name>`.

### Phase 2 — `project/`
`scan-project.ts`, `find-files.ts`, `read-file.ts`. **Exit:** correct file classification on a real repo; agent can request individual files.

### Phase 3 — `styling/` extraction
`analyze-styles.ts`, `analyze-responsive.ts`, `analyze-design-tokens.ts`. **Exit:** accurate extraction of real spacing/color/typography/breakpoint values in use, and a correctly identified (or correctly inferred) baseline.

### Phase 4 — `compare-baseline.ts`
The deviation engine itself. **Exit:** on a repo with intentionally-introduced inconsistencies, correctly flags the outliers and leaves consistent code alone. Watch false-positive rate closely — trust is the entire value proposition here.

### Phase 5 — `history/` + attribution
`git-history.ts`, joined against the deviation list. **Exit:** every flagged deviation is correctly attributed to the commit that introduced it. **This is your first real MVP milestone** — a working "here's what's drifted, and when it happened" tool.

### Phase 6 — `architecture/` (blast-radius context)
`analyze-imports.ts`, `analyze-components.ts`, `analyze-routes.ts`, `analyze-data-flow.ts`, plus `dependencies/analyze-dependencies.ts`. **Exit:** for a known deviation, ArchMap can correctly state how many components/pages depend on the affected file.

### Phase 7 — `architecture-agent`
Wire everything together; agent reasons about severity (blast radius × frequency × user-facing-ness) rather than just listing raw deviations. **Exit:** agent output prioritizes deviations sensibly — a drift in a shared, high-traffic component ranks above a one-off in an unused test page.

### Phase 8 — `architecture-workflow.ts`
Formalize the sequencing in Section 6 as an explicit, repeatable Mastra workflow. **Exit:** one call, given a repo URL, deterministically produces the same deviation report from the same repo state.

### Phase 9 — Output Layer
Turn the deviation report into something readable: a report, a simple dashboard, or (see Phase 10) a PR comment. **Exit:** someone unfamiliar with the repo can understand what's drifted and why it matters.

### Phase 10 — CI / PR Integration (the real product shape)
Scope the workflow to a PR diff instead of a full repo (using cached workspace + git diff), and post an automated PR comment/check summarizing new deviations with file/line/blame evidence. **Exit:** opening a PR against a connected repo produces an accurate automated comment — this is the point where ArchMap stops being a manual tool and becomes infrastructure a team keeps.

### Phase 11 — Change Tracking Over Time
Persist deviation reports as versioned snapshots keyed by commit SHA; diff snapshots to show drift trending up or down over time, team- or component-wide. **Exit:** "this component's drift score has doubled over the last month" style reporting.

### Phase 12 — Full Architecture Explanation (Optional, Lower Priority Now)
The original "explain this repo's architecture" output — graph + prose explanation of the whole system. Still valuable, but now explicitly secondary: build it once the deviation-detection core is solid, using the architecture tools already built in Phase 6.

